WEBVTT

00:03.920 --> 00:04.160
Okay.

00:04.160 --> 00:08.320
So what we're going to do with the dimension exploration, all that we have to do is to go and identify

00:08.560 --> 00:12.600
the unique values of each dimension that we have inside our database.

00:12.600 --> 00:17.600
This can help us to understand what are the categories, which countries, what are the product types

00:17.600 --> 00:19.120
that we have inside our database.

00:19.120 --> 00:21.200
And we have a very simple formula for that.

00:21.200 --> 00:27.040
So all what you need is the SQL keyword distinct together with any dimension in your data set like distinct

00:27.040 --> 00:29.080
country distinct category.

00:29.080 --> 00:33.160
So for example, if you are checking any column that is dimension, you can see a lot of values and

00:33.160 --> 00:34.000
repeating stuff.

00:34.000 --> 00:36.760
But now once you say distinct column, what's going to happen?

00:36.760 --> 00:39.200
You will get a list of all unique values.

00:39.200 --> 00:42.400
And with that, you can understand quickly how I have three different types.

00:42.400 --> 00:47.520
So I have A, B, C, and this is all going to help you to understand the granularity of your dimension.

00:47.520 --> 00:50.880
Does the dimension has like three values or 100 value.

00:50.880 --> 00:51.760
So it is very simple.

00:51.760 --> 00:53.960
Let's go and analyze our dimensions okay.

00:54.000 --> 00:57.520
So now let's explore the dimension values inside our database.

00:57.520 --> 00:59.680
So let's start with the first table that customers.

00:59.680 --> 01:04.360
And if you check those columns we have to find an interesting dimension like for example the country.

01:04.360 --> 01:09.480
So now what we can do we can go and explore all the countries our customers come from.

01:09.480 --> 01:10.680
So let's go and do that.

01:10.680 --> 01:12.560
It is very simple select distinct.

01:12.560 --> 01:17.330
And then we have our column the Dimension Country from our table customers.

01:17.690 --> 01:19.450
So let's go and execute it.

01:19.450 --> 01:22.170
And with that we can see in the result we have six countries.

01:22.170 --> 01:25.650
This is really nice in order to understand the geographical spread.

01:25.650 --> 01:31.010
So we have customers for our business that comes from six different countries Germany, United States,

01:31.010 --> 01:32.250
France, Canada and so on.

01:32.250 --> 01:35.810
So now with that, we have like the first little insights about our business.

01:35.810 --> 01:37.810
Now let's jump to another table.

01:37.810 --> 01:38.650
The products.

01:38.650 --> 01:44.090
So what you have to do is to explore all the categories inside our business, the major divisions.

01:44.250 --> 01:49.210
So we're going to say select distinct category from our table products.

01:49.210 --> 01:51.210
So let's go and execute it.

01:51.250 --> 01:53.410
Now in the output you can see we have four categories.

01:53.410 --> 01:56.770
We have the accessories bikes clothing and components.

01:56.770 --> 01:59.970
This is like giving us an overview of the product range.

01:59.970 --> 02:03.250
What are the major divisions inside our business now?

02:03.250 --> 02:06.130
The next one I'm digging deeper in this information.

02:06.130 --> 02:10.730
So not only I want to see the categories I would like as well to see the subcategories.

02:11.250 --> 02:15.650
I'm not starting a new query because there is of course a relationship between the category and the

02:15.650 --> 02:16.410
subcategory.

02:16.410 --> 02:17.930
Let's go now and execute it.

02:17.970 --> 02:23.490
Now you can see in the output our categories are now splitted into more specific groups.

02:23.490 --> 02:28.210
So for example the bikes over here we have mountain bikes, road bikes and so on.

02:28.210 --> 02:33.470
So as you can see the subcategories has more details about the products than their category.

02:33.510 --> 02:38.510
And now, in order to get the full picture, we're gonna bring now the product name.

02:39.350 --> 02:42.390
So with that, we're going to get a big picture in one shot.

02:42.430 --> 02:46.270
So now you can see the whole hierarchy of our products.

02:46.270 --> 02:50.910
And of course it is more interesting if you go and sort the data by those three informations.

02:51.030 --> 02:52.870
So let me just execute it again.

02:52.910 --> 02:57.030
So now if you go and explore our data for example we have here the category accessories.

02:57.030 --> 03:00.030
And we have a subcategory inside it called lights.

03:00.030 --> 03:02.950
And in this subcategory we have three different products.

03:02.950 --> 03:09.310
And if you scroll to the end of our table, you can see that we have around 295 products.

03:09.310 --> 03:14.270
So you can see the granularity of the product name is different than the category and the subcategory.

03:14.270 --> 03:17.470
And also three informations are related to each others.

03:17.510 --> 03:23.350
So now as you can see, after exploring those dimensions, we have now better understanding on how the

03:23.350 --> 03:24.510
data is organized.

03:24.510 --> 03:26.390
And this can help us by the analysis.

03:26.390 --> 03:30.390
If you are aggregating by the category, you will get only four rows.

03:30.430 --> 03:33.950
If you are aggregating by the products, you will get hundreds of rows.

03:33.950 --> 03:37.710
So this is how we explore the dimensions of our database.

03:37.750 --> 03:41.670
Okay, so now with that we have a clear picture about the dimensions inside our data sets.

03:41.710 --> 03:45.590
And now in the next step we are going to deep dive into one special type of dimensions.

03:45.590 --> 03:46.430
We have the dates.

03:46.430 --> 03:48.710
So we're going to explore the date columns.
